From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 12:24:15 +0200
Subject: net: dsa: tag_gswip3: add the GSWIP 3.0 tagging driver

GSWIP 3.0 carries a PMAC header that no existing tagging driver
describes. Like the GSWIP 2.x one it is prepended before the destination
MAC address with no EtherType marker, but it is eight bytes long in both
directions and every field the driver has to touch has moved: the source
port is the high nibble of byte 2 rather than three bits of byte 7, and
the destination port map is a 16-bit big endian value in bytes 6 and 7
rather than six bits of byte 3. The header also carries a 13-bit
sub-interface id and checksum-insertion offsets that GSWIP 2.x has no
room for. Neither tag_mxl-gsw1xx.c nor tag_mxl862xx.c fits either: both
insert at the EtherType position behind 0x88c3.

tag_mxl862xx.c is the closest, being the GSWIP 3.1 header, which agrees
in bytes 0, 1, 4 and 5. It diverges exactly where it matters, because
3.1 replaced the port map with an egress port index and a record id. So
this is a new driver rather than a parameter, taking the next free
protocol value.

Transmit sets the port map enable and the class enable, the two bits the
hardware needs to route by port map, names the destination port in the
map, and leaves the source port at the CPU value. It also records the
user port in the skb queue mapping, so that a conduit which reaches the
switch through more than one hardware queue can pick the right one.

Receive takes the source port from the header and strips it. Its two
early exits return NULL without freeing the socket buffer, because
dsa_switch_rcv() in this tree frees it when a tagger returns NULL and
freeing it here as well would double-free a buffer already returned to
the allocator. Neither exit is reachable while every frame decodes to a
port that has a network device, so the fault would be silent until a
frame named a port with none, and would then take a slab bug inside the
receive softirq.

offload_fwd_mark is left clear. Bytes 6 and 7 are a destination port map
on transmit only; on receive they carry the processor port's own bit, so
they say where the frame was delivered and not what the switch decided
about the rest of the fabric. The source-port field is the only other
forwarding information the header carries, and it records where the
frame came from. There is therefore nothing to condition the mark on,
and the bridge forwards in software.

